# TOEFL Reading Day56

## Topic: Water Scarcity

Water scarcity occurs when the demand for water exceeds the available supply or when water resources are difficult to access. It can result from natural conditions, population growth, pollution, and inefficient water management.

In dry regions, agriculture often consumes a large amount of water. As populations grow, cities and industries may also require more water. This can place pressure on rivers, lakes, and underground water sources.

Several strategies can help reduce water scarcity. These include improving irrigation systems, recycling wastewater, protecting wetlands, and reducing unnecessary consumption. However, successful water management requires cooperation among governments, communities, and individuals.

